        "answerEnglish": "A social audit is a participatory process through which citizens and communities examine the implementation and expenditure of government programmes. It helps determine whether benefits actually reach intended beneficiaries. Social audits promote transparency and accountability at the local level and can reveal irregularities, false beneficiaries, improper payments or implementation gaps. They are particularly important in welfare and rural development programmes. By involving beneficiaries directly, social audits make governance more participatory and strengthen public oversight."
